<title>Corollary 4.1.17 Similarity Preserves Betweeness</title>

    <p>
        4.1.16 states that collinearity is preserved in a similarity. Consider three collinear points A-B-C such that betweeness is not preserved.
        Without loss of generality, suppose: T(A)-T(C)-T(B). As segments are scaled uniformly in a similarity,
        <md>
            \|T(A)T(B)\| / \|AB\| = k = \|T(A)T(C)\| / \|AC\|
        </md>
        From the premise, 
        <md>
            \|AB\| \lt \|AC\|
        </md>
        As k &gt; 0, 
        <md>
         \|T(A)T(B)\| \lt \|T(A)T(C)\|
        </md> 
        but this contradicts T(A)-T(C)-T(B).
        Hence, collinearity preserves betweeness.
    </p>